NAME
    notify - put a dialog box in the operator's face (the intrusive `echo`)

SYNOPSIS
    notify
    notify <message> [--style <style>] [--title <title>]

DESCRIPTION
    TOS has two places to say something, and they are not interchangeable.

    The OUTPUT AREA, above the command line, is polite: it waits there until
    you next look down. That is right for results — a directory listing, a
    command's answer, a warning about something you just did.

    A DIALOG BOX is not polite. It is the DOS-style modal: double-line frame,
    title tab, drop shadow, buttons you must answer. It appears in the middle
    of the screen over whatever you were doing and blocks until you deal with
    it. That is right for things you cannot be allowed to miss.

    `notify` raises the second kind, on every seat.

        notify "Reactor maintenance in 5 minutes"
        notify "Backup finished" --style info
        notify "Coolant loop failed" --style danger --title REACTOR

    Styles are info (default), warn, danger, error, install, general — they
    pick the frame colour and default title, so a danger notice looks like
    one.

FOR PROGRAMS
    `notify` is the operator surface for kernel.notify, the facility any
    program can post to. A background service, an add-on's network handler
    and a sandboxed package command all run in contexts that cannot draw —
    another process may own the screen — so they post a notice and the shell
    that owns the display raises the box on its next idle tick.

    The Intercom rides this: an announcement at `alert` severity or above
    becomes a dialog box on every machine listening.

    A package asks for the `notify` capability in its manifest to use it:

        capabilities = { "notify" },

    which injects a two-function global — `notify.post(spec)` and
    `notify.result(id)`. The package cannot read the queue, dismiss another
    program's dialog, or reset the facility. The source shown on the box is
    stamped from the package name, not from anything the program supplies.

RATE LIMITS
    A modal any program can raise is a way to lock an operator out of their
    own computer — by accident (a service in a retry loop) or otherwise. So
    the facility enforces a floor that no caller can opt out of:

      * at least 10s between notices from the SAME source
      * at least 3s of quiet after ANY dialog is dismissed, before another
        may be raised — you always get your keyboard back
      * at most 8 notices waiting; past that, posting fails
      * a notice expires after 2 minutes unshown, so one raised while nobody
        was looking cannot ambush someone later

    A refused post is normal, not an error. The message is written to the
    kernel log either way, so nothing is lost — it simply does not get to
    interrupt. Programs layer their own policy on top (the Intercom's
    severity cooldown, for instance), never underneath.

    With no argument, `notify` prints how many notices are waiting and what
    the current limits are.

MULTI-SEAT
    Each seat raises each notice exactly once. The first operator to answer
    settles it for everybody — which is the right behaviour for "the facility
    is on fire" and harmless for anything quieter.

SEE ALSO
    echo, intercom, chat, log, doctor
